# Keep It Simple

This is a uni web project. Prefer straightforward solutions over clever or over-engineered ones.

## Code

- Use the simplest approach that works. Don't add abstractions "for later".
- One file, one job. Avoid deep folder trees and wrapper layers unless the project already uses them.
- Reuse plain patterns: simple functions, basic components, direct API calls.
- Match the stack: **Laravel + Blade + Bootstrap + MySQL** (plain CSS/JS). Do not switch to React/Vue/Tailwind unless asked.
- Don't introduce extra libraries unless they clearly save time (e.g. maps API for pickup location).

```php
// ❌ BAD — factory + strategy + config object for one form
$handler = DonationFormFactory::make($config)->handle($request);

// ✅ GOOD — a normal controller method
// Save new food listing
public function store(Request $request) {
    // validate, save, redirect
}
```

## Features

- Build what the requirements ask for first. Skip nice-to-haves until core flows work.
- Core flows: register/login → post donation → search/claim → track status → basic notifications.
- Prefer a working simple version over a half-finished complex one.

## UI/UX

- Clean layout, obvious buttons, readable labels. No fancy animations unless asked.
- Forms: only the fields needed (food name, category, quantity, expiry, address).
- Dashboards: show the important info first (active listings, nearby food, status).

## When Stuck

- Pick the option that is easier to explain in a viva or demo.
- If two approaches work, choose the one with less code and fewer moving parts.
